WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.560
Aah retirement.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.400
I could spend every day just like this, relaxing,
playing golf, spending time with family.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:18.120
But, like most of you, I can't retire right
now or really anytime soon.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.300
Even if we can't retire right now, there are
more than a few people who made their early

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.210
retirement dreams come true, no lottery ticket
or well-timed crypto investment required.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.500
Those people are part of a community called
FIRE.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.180
That's Financial Independence Retire Early.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.429
FIRE devotees combine high savings rates,
minimalist living and investing with low cost

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.039
index funds to reach retirement in relatively
short periods of time.

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:53.730
I talked to a guy named Bryce Leung who, along
with his wife, Christie, retired in their

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.970
early 30s and never looked back.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:02.329
We were given the same first prescriptive
script that our parents gave us for how to

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:03.329
live.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:07.329
Get a job, buy a house, go into debt, go get
a mortgage, spend the next 25 years it paying

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:11.800
off and then maybe, when you're 65, then you
can retire with a pension.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.340
The old rule book that they played with simply
doesn't apply for us anymore.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.820
How long was your journey once you began?

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.969
Nine years after we started working we retired.

00:01:20.969 --> 00:01:24.960
My wife was 31, I was 32 at the time, and
we said goodbye to our jobs forever and we've

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.189
been traveling ever since.

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:30.520
It's actually based, not on your age, but
how much money you have saved up versus how

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.530
much money you need to spend to maintain your
lifestyle.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.690
The rule that we use in the FIRE community
is called the 4% Rule.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.110
How much you have saved up versus how much
that you need to spend.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.020
If it's 4% of it, then you can retire safely
and never need to work again.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.630
Why is that number 4%?

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:52.659
In an academic study called The Trinity Study,
in which they back tested all of the stock

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:57.689
market history going back hundreds of years,
4% is the amount that it's safe to withdrawal

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:02.750
in that you won't run out of money over a
traditional retirement period.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:07.730
How did you change your lifestyles so that
you would live more frugally?

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.280
It was not so much a sacrifice of lifestyle
that got us here.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.890
It was avoiding the landmines that so many
people step on.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.390
The commonly accepted personal finance thing
of, you have to cut back until it's painful,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.580
no going out, no eating out and no drinking
coffees, and this kind of stuff.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.760
We didn't do any of that kind of stuff because
the biggest line item in a person's life is

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.530
housing, transportation and food.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.050
If you get those three right then the rest
of it almost doesn't even matter.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.760
But even if you can't retire at 31 like Bryce
did, there might still be some alterations

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.440
to your savings or spending that you can make
to retire at a more traditional age.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.330
Bloomberg reporter, Suzanne Woolley, is going
to tell us how to plan for that more traditional

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.240
retirement and the annoyances that we must
overcome to get there.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.510
Should people be scared about retiring?

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.450
Isn't everyone scared about retiring?

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.690
It's this so big a prospect.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:02:59.790
It's also uncertain.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:00.790
Yeah.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.210
And I think people get terrified by the idea
of, what is my number?

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.740
Do I need a million?

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.090
Do I need 2 million?

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.090
Who knows?

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.090
Right.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.090
And I think that can put people into paralysis.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.370
A lot of people don't even know how much they
spend each month.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.450
Just knowing what your budget is gives you
a sense of what you need to live on.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.680
What mistakes are people making as they plan
for retirement?

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.030
One mistake people might make is not planning
for contingencies.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:30.900
You have a point when you want to retire.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.470
Some people say, oh I want to retire when
I'm 62 or 65.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.470
Right.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.470
Well great.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.470
Yeah.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.470
But, life might not go your way.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.760
You might be laid off, there might be some
sort of ageism.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.370
You might get sick and not be able to work.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:47.040
So it's, when you're retirement planning,
you need to think through, how would I live

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.020
if I was laid off five years earlier than
I anticipated?

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.020
Right.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.820
And also, you need to have some sort of emergency
fund, maybe buckets.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.280
One is for short term and it's just cash,
like a year's living expenses, if you can

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.310
do that.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.460
The middle one is maybe in bonds and short
term investments.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.840
And the other part can be aggressive.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:16.750
The other bucket is more aggressive, really
long term, like 10 or 20 years.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:20.449
So if you want to get your FIRE dream started,
go ahead and make it happen.

00:04:20.449 --> 00:04:25.710
As for me, I'm going to do this for a few
more hours, then get back to work.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.490
Turn the camera off.

